# 🗄️ Universal Ignore Patterns

# --- Language Specific ---

# Node & Tooling
node_modules/
npm-debug.log*
yarn*.log
.pnpm-store/

# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
.venv
venv/
# Hypothesis example database (created by the local property-test run)
.hypothesis/
ENV/
build/
develop-eggs/
dist/
downloads/
eggs/
.eggs/
sdist/
wheels/
*.egg-info/
.installed.cfg
*.egg

# ⚠️ `lib/`, `lib64/`, `bin/`, `parts/` and `var/` ARE DELIBERATELY ABSENT.
#
# They were here as virtualenv leftovers, and `.venv` / `venv/` above already
# cover every one of them. Unanchored, they also matched at ANY depth - and
# they are SOURCE directories in mainstream ecosystems, not build output:
#
#   lib/   every Ruby gem and Elixir application keeps its source there,
#          as do many Node packages; SvelteKit's $lib root is src/lib/
#   bin/   Rails ships bin/rails and bin/setup; npm packages ship bin/cli.js
#
# `git add` on an ignored path succeeds and does nothing, so the file simply
# was not there - no warning, no error. In a scaffold whose whole premise is
# that YOU choose the directory structure, quietly deciding a tree is
# disposable is the one thing it must not do. A project that really does put
# build output in `bin/` should anchor it: `/bin/`.

# Go
obj/
*.exe
*.exe~
*.dll
*.so
*.dylib

# Rust
target/
**/*.rs.bk
cargo-home/

# Java / JVM
.gradle/
!src/main/**/build/
!src/test/**/build/
out/
.artifacts/
*.jar
*.war
*.ear
*.zip
*.tar.gz
*.rar

# C / C++
*.o
*.obj
*.a
*.lib
*.out
*.app
cmake-build-*/
.cache/

# Mobile (Android/iOS)
*.apk
*.aar
*.ipa
local.properties
.bundle/

# --- Environment & Secrets ---
# Always use .env.example for templates
#
# ⚠️ `.env.*` MATCHES EVERY VARIANT, and that is the point. Naming them one at
# a time missed .env.production, .env.staging, .env.development and .env.test
# - ordinary source files as far as git was concerned, while .env.example
# promised that every `.env*` was covered. A file holding real credentials
# must not depend on somebody having listed its exact name first.
#
# The negation keeps this repository's own example, and MUST stay after the
# pattern that would otherwise ignore it: git applies the last match.
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
*.pem
*.key
firebase-debug.log
.firebaserc

# --- IDE & Editors ---
# The four VS Code files a team shares. `tasks.json` belongs with the other
# three - it is where build, test and run tasks are defined, so leaving it out
# meant `git add -A` silently dropped it and teammates never got the tasks.
# The README beside them logs the folder, and without a line of its own here
# it would be dropped the same silent way.
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
!.vscode/settings.json
!.vscode/launch.json
!.vscode/tasks.json
!.vscode/README.md
.idea/
*.swp
*.swo
*~
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# --- Build & Caches ---
#
# `dist/`, `build/`, `out/` and `.cache/` are NOT repeated here: the language
# sections above already carry them. Restating a pattern after a negation
# silently revokes it - git applies the last match, so the `build/` that used
# to sit here undid `!src/main/**/build/` fifty lines earlier and swallowed
# any Java package named `build`. A duplicate is never harmless in this file.
coverage/
*.tsbuildinfo
.next/
.serverless/
.terraform/

# --- Docs Site (Jekyll / GitHub Pages) ---
# Local builds stay out of git. Nothing here publishes a docs site; wire that
# up yourself if you add one.
_site/
.jekyll-cache/
.jekyll-metadata

# --- Dotfiles: What Must Never Be Committed ---
#
# This repository is public, and it holds a home directory's configuration,
# which is where a machine keeps its keys, its tokens and a record of
# everything typed into it. Every rule here is unanchored or starts with
# `**/`, so it holds wherever a file lands in the tree.
#
# ⚠️ AN IGNORE RULE KEEPS A KNOWN FILE OUT, AND NOTHING ELSE. A token typed
# INTO a tracked file - an `export` in a shell rc - is caught by the secret
# scan instead, and by then it has been pushed. Put secrets in a `.local`
# file the tracked one sources (below), never in the tracked file itself.
#
# A file one of these catches by mistake gets a negation under its rule, not
# a `git add -f`, so the exception is written down where the next reader
# looks. test_dotfiles_gitignore.py holds every rule to both halves: what it
# must catch, and the file beside it that it must leave alone.

# SSH and GnuPG: everything in the folder stays out but its configuration,
# so a private key under ANY name is covered, not only id_rsa, and so are
# known_hosts, authorized_keys, the keyrings and the agent's sockets.
**/.ssh/*
!**/.ssh/config
!**/.ssh/config.d/
**/.gnupg/*
!**/.gnupg/*.conf

# Files that hold a token or a password in plain text. `credentials` alone
# is git's store, AWS, RubyGems and Cargo's old name; `credentials.*` is
# Cargo's new one, gcloud's database and Terraform's token file.
.netrc
.git-credentials
credentials
credentials.*
.pypirc
.vault-token
**/gh/hosts.yml
**/.docker/config.json
*.p12
*.pfx

# Tools that keep their tokens and account details beside their settings,
# so the whole folder stays out.
**/.aws/
**/.azure/
**/.kube/
**/.config/gcloud/
**/.config/github-copilot/
**/.config/configstore/

# Shell and REPL history: everything typed, including whatever was pasted
# into a prompt by mistake. XDG state is the same kind of file by
# definition - history, logs, undo - and never portable.
*_history
.zhistory
.histfile
.lesshst
.viminfo
.wget-hsts
.bash_sessions/
.zsh_sessions/
**/.local/state/

# Local overrides: one machine's settings, and the secrets a tracked file
# must not hold, kept in a file it sources - `.zshrc.local` by `.zshrc`.
# `?*` and not `*`, because `*` also matches nothing, and `*.local` would
# swallow the whole `.local/` folder along with them.
?*.local

# Machine-written zsh files: the completion dump, and anything compiled.
.zcompdump*
*.zwc

# --- The ALIEN Suite: What Stays On This Mac ---
#
# Each machine's own progress and settings, written beside the door. The
# suite runs on its defaults without them, and bootstrap.zsh writes the one
# switch it needs, so a fresh clone works as it is. The `.*` lines are their
# temp and lock files.
#
# ⚠️ A terminal.save BESIDE terminal.command WINS over the real one in
# ~/.local/state/alien, so a committed copy would take over on every machine
# that clones this repository, as well as publishing the progress in it.
terminal.save
terminal.save.*
terminal.conf
terminal.conf.*

# Working files: scratch from working on the suite, and backups.
Claude outputs/
_to_delete/
*.bak
